;;; A `:where` pair inside a pattern is dropped, so the rule fires without
;;; its guard — **W32**, register row `W32/where-in-pattern`
;;; (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two `p` facts and a rule whose premise writes `:where (neq ?x a)` inside
;;; the pattern `(p ?x …)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A term takes no keyword, and `:where` is on no form's list (Q-M1e2.28), so
;;; the program is refused at the parse, at the keyword: every command that
;;; reads it exits 1 (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `ec004a48` (the engine `916d97ac`'s), release and debug alike: the
;;; pair was dropped, so `r` fired for every `p`: root derived `(q a)` and
;;; `(q b)`, with no line (`ein saturate FILE --dump`), and R was four states
;;; over the candidates `(hit a)` and `(hit b)`, exit 0 (`ein solve FILE`,
;;; `ein oracle FILE`).
;;;
;;;

(relation p Node) (relation q Node) (relation hit Node)
(p a) (p b)
(rule r () :match (p ?x :where (neq ?x a)) :assert (q ?x))
